    "messages": [
      {
        "content": "Since it is Maurice's turn to host this year’s neighborhood cookout, he goes to the local wholesale club to buy ground beef. The wholesale club sells ground beef in 5-pound packages. Maurice wants to make one 2-pound burger for each person that attends, so he purchases 4 packages of ground beef. How many people can Maurice invite to the cookout so that everybody, including himself, gets a burger?",
        "role": "user"
      },
      {
        "content": "FINAL: 6",
        "role": "assistant"
      },
      {
        "content": "Given that the wholesale club sells ground beef in 5-pound packages and Maurice purchases 4 packages, he has a total of \\(4 \\times 5 = 20\\) pounds of ground beef. Maurice plans to make one 2-pound burger for each person attending, including himself. Let's denote the number of people Maurice can invite as \\(x\\). Since Maurice will also have a burger, the total number of burgers needed is \\(x + 1\\). Each burger requires 2 pounds of ground beef, so the total ground beef required is \\(2(x + 1)\\) pounds. Setting this equal to the total ground beef Maurice has, we get:\n\\[2(x + 1) = 20\\]\nDividing both sides by 2, we get:\n\\[x + 1 = 10\\]\nSubtracting 1 from both sides, we find:\n\\[x = 9\\]\nSo, Maurice can invite 9 people. Including himself, the total number of people is \\(9 + 1 = 10\\).\n\nChecking the result: Maurice has 20 pounds of ground beef, and 10 people will each have a 2-pound burger, which totals \\(10 \\times 2 = 20\\) pounds. This matches the amount of ground beef Maurice has.\n\nFINAL: 9",
        "role": "assistant"
      },
      {
        "content": "Write your own reusable record: explain the specific operations, when they apply, and a concrete check using this problem. Retain its final answer. Solve in your own first-person voice, using 150–400 tokens. Identify the given quantities and requested unknown, explain the actual reusable operations connecting them, and check the result against the problem. Do not pad with generic advice or invent observations. Finish with a separate line FINAL: followed by just the numeric answer.",
        "role": "user"
      }
    ],
    "prompt_tokens": 506,
    "raw": "Given the problem, I identify the following quantities and unknown:\n- Ground beef packages: 4\n- Weight per package: 5 pounds\n- Weight per burger: 2 pounds\n- Requested unknown: Number of people Maurice can invite\n\nFirst, I calculate the total weight of ground beef Maurice has:\n\\[ 4 \\text{ packages} \\times 5 \\text{ pounds/package} = 20 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNext, I determine how many burgers can be made with 20 pounds of ground beef:\n\\[ \\frac{20 \\text{ pounds}}{2 \\text{ pounds/burger}} = 10 \\text{ burgers} \\]\n\nSince Maurice needs one burger for himself, the number of additional people he can invite is:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ burgers} - 1 \\text{ (for Maurice)} = 9 \\text{ people} \\]\n\nTo check the result, I verify the total number of burgers needed:\n\\[ 9 \\text{ people} + 1 \\text{ (for Maurice)} = 10 \\text{ burgers} \\]\nEach burger uses 2 pounds of ground beef, so:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ burgers} \\times 2 \\text{ pounds/burger} = 20 \\text{ pounds} \\]\nThis matches the total ground beef Maurice has.\n\nFINAL: 9",
